# nuxt-cms-core Project Rules

## Project Philosophy

- This project is a reusable CMS Core for Nuxt 4.
- The CMS is designed for content management, not page building.
- Developers build website structure in code.
- The database stores editable content only.
- Business logic belongs to client projects.
- CMS Core contains only reusable functionality.
- Keep the architecture simple, maintainable and scalable.
- Avoid overengineering.

---

## Architecture

- Use Nuxt Layer + Nuxt Module architecture.
- CMS Core must remain framework-independent except for Nuxt integration.
- Database provider must be replaceable.
- Never tightly couple the project to Supabase.
- All data access must go through repositories.
- Components must never communicate with Supabase directly.
- Keep clear boundaries between Core and Client Project.

---

## CMS Boundaries

CMS Core MAY contain:

- Admin panel
- Edit Mode
- Authentication
- Media Library
- Forms
- Localization
- Generic CMS Components
- Generic Repositories
- Generic Composables
- Generic Utilities

CMS Core MUST NOT contain:

- Business logic
- Business entities
- Product logic
- Ecommerce logic
- Landing page structure
- Client specific UI
- Project specific repositories

---

## Page Structure

The CMS is NOT a page builder.

Never:

- store page layout in database
- store component hierarchy in database
- generate dynamic page structures
- generate drag & drop builders

Developers always create page structure manually.

Users may only:

- edit existing content
- create template pages
- manage collections when explicitly supported

---

## Components

CMS Components must remain generic.

Allowed examples:

- CmsText
- CmsImage
- CmsButton
- CmsCollection

Never generate:

- Hero components
- Product cards
- Landing sections
- Business specific components

Business components belong to the Client Project.

---

## Data Access

Always use Repository Pattern.

Flow:

UI

↓

Composable

↓

Repository

↓

Database Adapter

Never bypass repositories.

Repositories hide implementation details.

---

## Database

Prefer simple database design.

Content only.

No page structure.

No UI structure.

Collections store data only.

Database implementation must remain replaceable.

---

## Migrations

Create migrations only when database schema changes.

Prefer one coherent migration per feature.

Avoid many tiny migrations.

Do not generate unnecessary migrations.

Prefix every Core table, policy and constraint with `cms_` (MASTER_PLAN C-38): Core shares the `public` schema with Client Project tables.

Until the first npm publish, fix a feature's existing migration in place instead of adding a follow-up migration, then run `supabase db reset`. After publish, applied migrations are never edited: changes go into a new migration (C-31).

Never rely on default Data API grants: revoke from `anon` and `authenticated`, then grant only what the feature needs.

---

## Development Rules

Never:

- create git commits
- push code
- create pull requests
- rename unrelated files
- refactor unrelated code
- install dependencies without asking
- remove existing functionality unless requested

Implement only the requested feature.

Reuse existing architecture whenever possible.

Modify existing code before creating new abstractions.

---

## Code Style

Use:

- Composition API
- TypeScript
- readable code
- explicit naming
- small focused functions

Avoid:

- any
- magic values
- unnecessary abstractions
- duplicated code

Prefer readability over clever code.

---

## Performance

Public website must not load:

- Edit Mode
- Editor UI
- Admin code
- unnecessary JavaScript

Use lazy loading whenever possible.

Avoid unnecessary watchers.

Optimize for minimal runtime overhead.

---

## UI

Keep UI minimal.

Prefer project components.

Avoid unnecessary dependencies.

Avoid heavy UI frameworks.

Use native CSS: CSS variables (UI kit tokens) and scoped CSS.

Never use Tailwind or SCSS in Core. Client Projects may use Tailwind for their own site.

Admin tokens are prefixed `--cms-` and live under `.cms-admin`, never on `:root`.

Admin UI translations go through `useAdminI18n()` with `cms.*` keys
(dictionaries in `app/admin/locales`). Never use vue-i18n or `@nuxtjs/i18n` in Core,
and do not add a root `i18n/` directory: it is the default `@nuxtjs/i18n` directory.

---

## AI Behaviour

Before implementing:

- understand existing architecture
- reuse existing patterns
- search existing code first

If requirements are unclear:

ASK.

Do not invent missing requirements.

Do not invent database schema.

Do not invent architecture.

Do not silently introduce new patterns.

Explain major architectural decisions before implementation.

Prefer consistency over novelty.

Think as a senior software engineer.

Optimize for maintainability.

---

# Testing Rules

Testing should remain lightweight.

Prefer manual testing during development.

Write automated tests only for critical CMS functionality.

Avoid generating tests for simple UI components.

Avoid snapshot tests.

Do not generate E2E tests unless explicitly requested.

Do not create tests only to increase test coverage.

Focus testing on:

- repositories
- business logic
- utility functions
- critical CMS workflows

Testing should never significantly slow down development.

# Repository & File Naming

Follow consistent naming conventions.

Repositories:

- PagesRepository
- MediaRepository
- FormsRepository

Composables:

- useCms()
- usePage()
- useMedia()
- useAuth()

Components:

- PascalCase
- CmsText
- CmsImage
- CmsButton

Plugins:

- cms.client.ts
- cms.server.ts

Middleware:

- kebab-case

Utilities:

- camelCase

Avoid inconsistent naming.

# Nuxt Conventions

Follow Nuxt 4 conventions whenever possible.

Prefer built-in Nuxt features over custom solutions.

Use:

- auto imports
- server routes
- composables
- runtime config
- plugins
- middleware

Avoid reinventing existing Nuxt functionality.

Use project aliases consistently.

Prefer Composition API.

Keep components focused and reusable.

# Supabase Rules

Supabase is the current backend implementation.

The architecture must always allow replacing Supabase in the future.

Never expose service_role keys.

Never access Supabase directly from UI components.

Always use repositories.

Use Row Level Security.

Keep Storage independent from business logic.

Keep migrations clean.

Prefer one migration per feature.

Avoid migration noise.

Avoid unnecessary SQL complexity.

Prefer database simplicity over clever SQL.

Do not duplicate data unless necessary.

Keep database portable for future backend replacement.

# Dependencies

Prefer built-in Nuxt and Vue functionality.

Avoid adding new dependencies without clear benefit.

Before introducing a dependency:

- verify that existing project code cannot solve the problem
- prefer lightweight solutions
- discuss large dependencies before installation

Minimize project dependencies.

# Refactoring

Do not refactor code unless requested.

Do not rewrite working code for style only.

Improve existing implementations incrementally.

Avoid large architectural rewrites.

Prefer evolution over replacement.

# Simplicity

Always choose the simplest solution that satisfies current requirements.

Do not implement future features before they are needed.

Avoid speculative abstractions.

Avoid solving problems that do not exist yet.

Optimize for clarity and maintainability.

## Future Compatibility

Always keep the architecture ready for:

- replacing Supabase with another backend
- reusable npm package distribution
- future CLI support
- future project updates

Never introduce decisions that make backend replacement difficult.

---

These rules have higher priority than implementation convenience.

If a request conflicts with these rules, ask for clarification before implementing.